In Vue.js v2.x, when using the v-for directive to iterate over a range, the starting value is automatically set to 1 and increments by 1. Unfortunately, there is no built-in feature to specify a custom starting value for the range iteration. 

However, you can achieve this by using a computed property to generate the range with the desired starting value. Here's an example:

```javascript
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      range: []
    }
  },
  computed: {
    customRange() {
      return Array.from({length: 10}, (value, index) => index + 5)
    }
  },
  template: `
    <div>
      <span v-for="n in customRange">{{ n }}</span>
    </div>
  `
}
```

In this example, the `customRange` computed property generates an array from 5 to 14 (inclusive), effectively starting the iteration at 5. The `v-for` directive then iterates over this custom range, displaying the desired page numbers.

This approach allows you to maintain control over the starting value of your range iteration, providing flexibility for your specific use case.

