In PL/SQL, how can I call a procedure that has an OUT parameter without assigning it to a variable? If a procedure has an OUT parameter, you must assign its value to a variable in the calling block. This is because the OUT parameter is used to return a value from the procedure to the calling block. If you don't care about the return value, you can still declare a variable to receive it, but you can ignore it afterwards. Here's an example:

```sql
DECLARE
  dummy_variable VARCHAR2(100);
BEGIN
  my_procedure('param1', dummy_variable);
  -- You can ignore dummy_variable here
END;
```

In this example, `dummy_variable` is declared to receive the OUT parameter from `my_procedure`, but since you don't need the value, you can simply ignore it. This ensures that the procedure executes successfully without raising any exceptions.

