In C++ you can use constructor overloading to provide multiple constructors with different parameters. Each constructor will have the same name as the class, but different parameters. The compiler will automatically select the correct constructor based on the arguments provided. Here's an example:

```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { /* ... */ } // Default constructor
    MyClass(int value) { /* ... */ } // Constructor with one parameter
    MyClass(int value1, int value2) { /* ... */ } // Constructor with two parameters
};
```

In this example, `MyClass()` is the default constructor, `MyClass(int value)` is a constructor that takes one `int` parameter, and `MyClass(int value1, int value2)` is a constructor that takes two `int` parameters. When you create an instance of `MyClass`, the compiler will choose the appropriate constructor based on the arguments you provide.

