One way to avoid code duplication with multiple constructors is to use a factory method. This approach involves creating a static method within the class that decides which constructor to call based on the input parameters. By doing so, you centralize the logic for choosing the appropriate constructor, reducing redundancy and making the code more maintainable. Here's a simple example:

```java
public class MyClass {
    private String value;

    // Traditional constructors
    public MyClass(String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public MyClass(int id, String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    // Factory method
    public static MyClass createWithValue(String value) {
        return new MyClass(value);
    }

    public static MyClass createWithIdAndValue(int id, String value) {
        return new MyClass(id, value);
    }

    // Other methods and fields...
}
```

In this example, `createWithValue` and `createWithIdAndValue` are factory methods that decide which constructor to use based on the input parameters. This way, you avoid duplicating the logic of handling different constructor signatures across multiple constructors. The factory methods provide a single point of control for creating instances of the class, making the code cleaner and easier to manage.

